 ## Overview
-Lit sphere shading is a non-photorealistic rendering technique that captures artistic lighting styles by using an image of a sphere, shaded in a desired style, as a lookup table [S1][S2, unofficial]. It represents the shading of an object as a function of eye-space normal orientation, allowing the sphere to act as a proxy object for illumination [S1][S2, unofficial]. Unlike physical illumination models, it decouples lighting from light transport equations, relying instead on view-dependent mapping [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is distinct from [Blinn-Phong Toon Shading](4708-blinn-phong-toon-shading.md) and [Volumetric Toon Shading](5276-volumetric-toon-shading.md) because it does not compute lighting analytically per fragment but rather samples a pre-rendered or extracted artistic style map [S1].
